== Steps of Project Portfolio Management Process

+ *Define Business Objectives:* Set clear goals for the portfolio.
+ *Collect Project Ideas:* Gather possible projects that fit the objectives.
+ *Select the Best Projects:* Choose projects based on cost, benefit, risk, NPV, etc.
+ *Check Feasibility:* Make sure the selected projects are possible and realistic.
+ *Execute and Manage:* Implement, monitor, and control the selected projects together.

== PMI (Project Management Institute)

PMI is a global professional organization that provides standards, guidelines, and best practices for project management. It helps project managers plan, execute, monitor, and complete projects successfully.

=== PMI Framework
The PMI Framework is a set of standard processes, knowledge areas, tools, and techniques used to manage projects effectively.

It helps project managers to:
- Reduce project risks.
- Achieve project goals.
- Manage resources properly.
- Coordinate different activities.
- Complete projects on time, within budget, and with required quality.

== Main Project Management Processes

A simple way to remember the major process groups:

- *S* -- Starting (Initiation)
- *P* -- Planning
- *E* -- Keeping/Executing
- *C* -- Monitoring & Controlling
- *T* -- Termination (Closing)

*Example: Construction Project*
+ *Initiation:* Define the requirements for the building.
+ *Planning:* Schedule activities, estimate costs, and allocate resources.
+ *Execution:* Construct the building and manage workers.
+ *Monitoring & Controlling:* Check progress, budget, and quality.
+ *Closing:* Complete final inspection and hand over the building.
== Project Success 
Project success is when a project meets its goals, is completed on time, stays within budget, and delivers the expected quality. It also means that the project satisfies the needs of stakeholders and contributes to the organization’s overall objectives.
=== Key Drivers of Project Success

+ *Clear Objectives and Scope*
  - Define exactly what the project must achieve.
  - *Example:* Building a school with a fixed number of classrooms.
+ *Strong Leadership and Stakeholder Engagement*
  - Good leadership and involvement of stakeholders keep everyone aligned.
  - *Example:* Regular meetings between the contractor and school board.
+ *Effective Planning and Resource Allocation*
  - Properly manage time, money, workers, and materials.
  - *Example:* Assign enough workers to complete the walls on time.
+ *Risk Management*
  - Identify possible problems early and prepare solutions.
  - *Example:* Having backup suppliers for construction materials.
+ *Monitoring, Quality, and Closure*
  - Regularly check progress and quality and properly close the project.
  - *Example:* Inspecting classrooms before handing the building over.

These drivers ensure the project is completed on time, within budget, and with expected quality.

=== Inhibitors of Project Success

Inhibitors are factors that can cause project failure, delay, increased cost, or poor quality.

+ *Poor Communication:* Team members and stakeholders do not share information clearly.
+ *Lack of Leadership:* There is no strong guidance or direction for the project.
+ *Unclear Expectations:* Project goals, roles, and responsibilities are not clear.
+ *Poor Planning:* Tasks, schedules, resources, and activities are not properly organized.
+ *Negative Attitude Towards IT:* Team members resist using new technology or systems.
+ *Changing Business Strategies:* Frequent changes in organizational goals can disturb the project.
+ *Weak Top Management Support:* Senior managers do not provide enough guidance, approval, or resources.
+ *Conflicting Objectives:* Different departments or stakeholders have different goals.
+ *Inadequate Resources:* There is a shortage of people, money, equipment, or materials.
+ *Financial Limitations:* Budget restrictions can slow down or stop project activities.
+ *Lack of Historical Data:* Lack of past project information makes planning and decision-making difficult.

== Knowledge Areas of PMI
+ *Stakeholder Needs & Expectations*
  - Focuses on understanding and managing the interests of anyone affected by the project such as employees, customers, suppliers, government, and local communities.

+ *Core Knowledge Areas* (Main parts of a project):
  - *Scope:* What the project will do.
  - *Time:* Project schedule.
  - *Cost:* Project budget.
  - *Quality:* Required standards and deliverables.

+ *Facilitating Knowledge Areas* (Support the main project activities):
  - *Human Resources:* Managing team members.
  - *Communication:* Sharing project information.
  - *Risk:* Problems that may arise.
  - *Procurement:* Purchasing required materials and services.

+ *Integrative Knowledge Area*
  - Combines and coordinates all parts of the project.
  - Ensures different activities work together smoothly.

+ *Tools and Techniques*
  - Methods used to manage projects effectively (e.g., project management software, schedules, charts, checklists, spreadsheets, or paper and pencil).

== Types of Project Portfolio Management (PPM) Users

+ *Execution-Focused PPM Users*
  - Focus on day-to-day project operations, tracking progress, monitoring costs, and preparing reports.
  - *Example:* A project manager checking whether tasks are completed on time and within budget.

+ *Portfolio-Level PPM Users*
  - Focus on the overall portfolio, deciding which projects to include, planning overall delivery, and reviewing results.
  - *Example:* Senior management selecting the best combination of projects for the organization.

== Project Management Office (PMO)

A PMO is a team or department that ensures project management standards are followed, provides support, and aligns projects with organizational goals. It can be *Internal* (part of the organization) or *External* (provided by an outside organization).

*Benefits of PMO:*
+ *Standardization:* Provides common processes and methods.
+ *Better Resource Utilization:* Uses time, money, and people efficiently.
+ *Better Monitoring and Control:* Tracks progress and performance.
+ *Risk Reduction:* Reduces risks using proven best practices.
+ *Strategic Alignment:* Ensures projects support company goals.
+ *Better Communication:* Improves reporting among stakeholders.
+ *Faster Project Delivery:* Helps projects finish on time.

=== Types of PMO
+ *Supportive PMO:* Provides templates, training, and guidelines (Low control; "Supports").
+ *Controlling PMO:* Checks compliance, procedures, and reports (Moderate control; "Checks").
+ *Directive PMO:* Directly manages projects, assigns managers, and provides strong direction (High control; "Directly manages").

=== Functions of PMO
- Develop and maintain project management standards and methods.
- Provide training, tools, and templates.
- Support resource allocation across projects.
- Monitor project progress and provide reports.
- Improve communication among project stakeholders.
- Align projects with organizational strategy and goals.
- Facilitate risk management and quality control.
- Prepare for disaster recovery in projects.
- Manage or assist in Project Portfolio Management (PPM).